Task: Define the parser runtime-configuration contract (Phase 1, FR-3)

Files modified:
- server/src/services/ReleaseParserProvider.ts

Summary:
Added the runtime-configuration contract for ReleaseParser: the
ReleaseParserRuntimeConfig interface, four exported defaults
(DEFAULT_PARSE_TIMEOUT_MS 15s, DEFAULT_BATCH_TIMEOUT_MS 30s,
DEFAULT_FILES_TIMEOUT_MS 45s, DEFAULT_MAX_CONCURRENCY 4), the
KNOWN_FAST_MODELS allowlist, and resolveReleaseParserRuntimeConfig().

Why:
The deadlines were three magic numbers inside ReleaseParser.ts. The
shipped default model (minimax/minimax-m2.7) measured 77-88s per call
against a 15s/20s deadline - 4-6x slower than its own timeout - so every
call aborted and parse() fell to the 36-line regex while parseBatch()
returned []. The failure was invisible because both fallbacks are silent.
Making the deadlines configuration, defaulting them with headroom over a
measured-fast model, and recording the measurement table beside the
allowlist is what stops that recurring undetected.

Design notes:
- readPositiveIntEnv treats 0, negatives, non-integers and non-numerics as
  absent. A zero deadline would abort every call instantly, which would
  reproduce the original defect via configuration.
- Batch (30s) and files (45s) defaults are raised above the old 20s because
  they carry 25 and 50 items respectively; the measured 2.4-3.1s was for 8
  titles, so headroom scales with batch size.
- KNOWN_FAST_MODELS drives a warning, not a rejection. Operators keep the
  OPENROUTER_MODEL override; they just cannot use it to silently
  reintroduce the defect.

